- How to use `az` command map:
  - Issue lifecycle:
  - `az issue list --limit 20`, `az issue list --status in_progress --limit 20`, `az issue list --query "text" --updated-after YYYY-MM-DD --limit 20`, `az issue search --status open --limit 20 --query "text"` (or `az issue search --status open --limit 20 "text"`) searches title, description, notes, design, acceptance, labels, and implementations; `az issue get <issue-id>`, `az issue create "Child task" [--json]` (immediate child work; auto-parents under `AZEDARACH_ISSUE_ID`){{ if .TmuxAvailable }}, `az issue split "Child task" [--json]` (create isolated child session){{ end }}, `az issue create --deferred "Later backlog task" [--json]` (standalone backlog only; skips auto-parenting and is not a worktree/session control), `az issue update <issue-id> --status in_review|in_progress|open [--json]`, `az issue close <issue-id> [--json]` (integrate, clean up, and close)
  - Proper issue creation: never leave an issue as just a title. Use `az issue create --title "Specific outcome" --description "Context, scope, acceptance/validation notes"` or immediately follow title-only creation with `az issue update <issue-id> --description "..."` before starting work or fanout.
  - Start-issue wording: when the user says "start an az issue", "add/start an issue", or "create and start an issue", create or identify the issue and then run `az session start <issue-id>`. Do not treat `az issue update <issue-id> --status in_progress` as starting a session unless the user explicitly asks to mark/update status.
  - `az issue update`: use `--update-impl` only when changing implementation assignments; status/title/notes updates do not need it.
  - Status semantics: use `open` for not-yet-active work, `in_progress` while actively working, `in_review` when implementation is complete and waiting for human/orchestrator review or integration, and `closed` only after acceptance/integration is complete.
  - Blocked is not an issue status. Represent blocked work with dependency edges (`az issue dep add <blocked-issue> <blocker-issue> --type blocks`) and record blocker evidence in issue notes or mailbox events.
  - `az issue bulk-create --input issues.json --json` (create many issues, epics, or nested `children` trees from JSON when shaping a graph up front), `az issue bulk-update --input updates.json --json`, `az issue update <issue-id> --design "..."`, `az issue update <issue-id> --notes "..."`, `az issue update <issue-id> --append-notes "..."`
  - Reports and attachments:
  - `az issue document add <issue-id> <file>`, `az issue document list <issue-id>`, `az issue document remove <issue-id> <attachment-id>`
  - Markdown attachments render in the TUI; other file types can be opened externally.
  - Child issue creation without worker launch:
  - Batch planning/catalog fanout: `az issue bulk-create --input issues.json --json` with `parent_id` or nested `children` creates parented child issues without starting sessions or orchestration.
  - Single tracking-only child: `az issue create "Child task" [--json]` auto-parents under `AZEDARACH_ISSUE_ID`; for another parent, create the issue and attach it with `az issue dep add <child-id> <parent-id> --type parent-child`.
  - Worker fanout/session launch: `az issue split --parent <issue-id> "Child task"` can create the child and start orchestration/session work; use `az issue split` only when you intentionally want isolated worker fanout.
  - Issue dependency graph:
  - `az issue dep add <issue-id> <depends-on-id> [--type blocks|related|parent-child|discovered-from|created-in] [--json]`, `az issue dep remove <issue-id> <depends-on-id> [--type blocks|related|parent-child|discovered-from|created-in] [--confirm] [--json]`
  - Issue graph/root membership comes only from auto-parenting with `AZEDARACH_ISSUE_ID` or explicit `parent-child` dependency edges. Do not use `--impl <id>` when you mean "parent this under <id>"; `--impl` is implementation metadata only and never attaches an issue to a graph/root.
  - If a child issue was requested and `az issue create --json` returns `parent_id: ""`, stop before launching work: verify the intended root with `az issue get <intended-root>`, then attach the child with `az issue dep add <child-id> <intended-root> --type parent-child` or create a fresh child from the correct active issue context.
  - Parent-child edges are hierarchy/board-nesting edges, not readiness controls. Do not remove a parent-child edge to pause, derank, or supersede orchestration child work; keep the child under its parent, set it `open` when appropriate, and add a `blocks` edge to the blocker/design issue instead. Removing a parent-child edge from active child work requires explicit parent-orphan confirmation.
